WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.000
We all knew it, but now there is proof: there&nbsp;
are some clear COVID-19 winners – and losers.&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.080
An Oxfam study shows that the world's 10 richest&nbsp;
men doubled their fortunes during the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.440
while more than 160 million&nbsp;
people are projected to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.680
been pushed into poverty since the pandemic hit.&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.160
But what if I told you that this doesn’t&nbsp;
have to be the trajectory we’re going down?&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.880
Let’s take a look at why the world has become&nbsp;
even more divided and dispersed in the past two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.920
years – and what we can do against this rising&nbsp;
inequality. Because there are actually lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.320
solutions floating around, including from&nbsp;
the very people who increased their wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.560
during the pandemic, now asking to be&nbsp;
taxed properly for the greater good.&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.240
We've really had during the&nbsp;
pandemic an inequality explosion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.280
It's been a period that has been an&nbsp;
unprecedented boom for billionaires.&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.000
It's a question of resource distribution.&nbsp;
There's enough resources on this planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.760
but the distribution doesn't work.
It’s not a secret that the world has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.960
always been divided into the poor and rich. But&nbsp;
while we are on a good way to closing this gap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.200
the pandemic has been a huge setback.
One of the reasons is that governments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.280
across the world cut interest rates to&nbsp;
record low levels during the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:23.120
while central banks bought up government bonds and&nbsp;
stocks in order to increase economic activity. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:28.240
of that led to soaring stock markets, benefiting&nbsp;
those who already had money and investments.&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.600
It was hard not to make any money on shares&nbsp;
and speculation within the last two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.480
because the entire market was going up&nbsp;
and this was because of the liquidity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:46.560
support of all central banks worldwide.
Meanwhile, the rest of the world – and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.560
especially those who couldn’t work from home&nbsp;
- experienced dramatic income losses during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.760
the pandemic. And it was especially those who were&nbsp;
already poor and vulnerable who suffered the most.&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:01.760
Urban informal sector workers have been affected&nbsp;
more than formal sector workers. Women have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.800
affected more than men. And workers with less&nbsp;
education have been affected much more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.440
workers with more education, just because of&nbsp;
the nature of work that they're engaged in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.200
A lot of these people are in the service sectors&nbsp;
which are basically client-facing services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.760
which were the first ones to be affected&nbsp;
when economies closed down and mobility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.080
was restricted because of the pandemic.
So there needs to be support for those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:32.080
groups which have suffered the most during&nbsp;
the pandemic. And it includes policies like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.760
access to finance, access to training, in some&nbsp;
cases providing support to households and so on.&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.240
Data from the World Bank shows that the&nbsp;
poorest 20 percent of the globe had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.440
steepest decline in incomes. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
they are also the ones to recover the slowest.&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.920
As jobs are coming back, it's not as if all&nbsp;
women who have lost jobs are coming back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.040
at the same rate as male workers are.
The poorest 40 percent have not started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.560
to recover their income losses, whereas the top&nbsp;
40 percent have actually recovered quite a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.960
of their initial income losses. So the difference&nbsp;
in recovery essentially results in the gap between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.960
the top and the bottom widening over time.
One field that is responsible for a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.280
rise in inequality and a slower&nbsp;
recovery over time is education.&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.120
A large fraction of children in poorer families,&nbsp;
particularly those in low-income countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.160
have not had access to learning during&nbsp;
long school closures amidst the pandemic.&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.800
Data by the World Bank suggests that the share of&nbsp;
10-year-olds who cannot read a basic text could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.120
reach 70 percent in low- and middle-income&nbsp;
countries because of school closures.&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:47.040
The implications of that for income distribution&nbsp;
in the future, I think, are more severe than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.600
anything that happened to jobs and incomes now.
One of the biggest reasons for the slow economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.920
recovery in the global south has been&nbsp;
the distribution of COVID-19 vaccines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.560
While only 7% of people in low-income&nbsp;
countries have received a vaccine dose, more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:06.080
than 75% have done so in high-income countries.
One idea that is already being discussed globally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:11.200
is a billionaires’ income tax or a wealth tax.&nbsp;
The money could be used to invest in worldwide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.080
vaccine production as well as health care in&nbsp;
the fight against the pandemic – and inequality.&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.320
And believe it or not, some ultra-rich&nbsp;
people themselves are all for it.&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.560
Over 50 millionaires and billionaires in&nbsp;
German-speaking countries have banded together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.040
in a group called “Tax Me Now”, demanding&nbsp;
to be taxed properly for the greater good.&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.240
I think it's very important that the class&nbsp;
of millionaires and even richer people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.600
stand for tax justice.
We're facing a once-in-a-century challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:45.840
right now. We are facing ecological disaster,&nbsp;
the failure of democracies, the rise of populism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:53.440
and the large inequality crisis and all of&nbsp;
these challenges are linked to questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:00.240
of resource allocation and there are enough&nbsp;
resources. So we as the rich people’s class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:06.880
we can choose if we pay our fair share or if we&nbsp;
face things like revolution and social unrest.&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.800
Tax Me Now wants the rich to be taxed more on a&nbsp;
national level, and to fight against tax havens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.520
on an international level by introducing&nbsp;
measurements and minimum taxation standards.&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:27.360
So my average tax level is lower than the tax&nbsp;
level of my employees. That's not okay. I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:35.120
assure you that we have a lot of privileges right&nbsp;
now that we shouldn't have, like we are not being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:41.520
taxed for heritage. We're not properly&nbsp;
taxed for capital gains. We have a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:49.280
of loopholes and possibilities to evade taxes on&nbsp;
a legal basis. Frankly speaking, it's too easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:57.440
to evade taxation on an international basis,&nbsp;
and these things at least shouldn't be legal.&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:03.280
But how exactly would it work to change that&nbsp;
on a global scale? We all know how easy it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.320
for millionaires to maneuver their money – or the&nbsp;
headquarters of their companies - to tax havens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.120
such as Switzerland, Luxembourg, the&nbsp;
Cayman Islands, Hong Kong or Singapore.&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.720
It has to be done through international&nbsp;
cooperation. Otherwise, they will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.840
able to just move it around, right?&nbsp;
So, [there’s a need for] sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:25.920
establishing minima that prevent certain&nbsp;
countries from trying to act as tax havens.&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.120
And of course, the minimum corporate tax&nbsp;
that's being agreed at the OECD and now has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:39.120
to be ratified is a start in that direction. It&nbsp;
shows that with political will, it can be done.&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.880
What we think about is, for example, a&nbsp;
property register. We believe 30 percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:48.000
of all the property of the billionaires&nbsp;
and millionaires at the present is being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:55.280
hidden away in international tax havens.
So long as money is liquid and is flowing around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:07:01.520
as money, it's flowing through the banking system.&nbsp;
The banking system has a stake in the economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:08.560
and it can be affected by it, by the government.&nbsp;
Different governments can seriously constrain what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:15.840
banks do. They just need to want to do that.
Rich people are very good at lobbying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:22.720
and they have a lot of backing in government.&nbsp;
And so greater attention paid to this by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:30.320
everybody else, the 99 percent if you like, can&nbsp;
play a real role in changing the political game.&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:34.720
That’s of course easier said than done. We&nbsp;
know from experience that coordinated global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:39.680
action on issues like this is often slow&nbsp;
and patchy and doesn’t happen overnight.&nbsp;

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.520
That’s why some initiatives aren't&nbsp;
directing their attention to only the rich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:47.440
but also the global middle class.
Many of us, especially those living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.920
in developed nations, are actually richer&nbsp;
than we may think – and by donating just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:56.880
a fraction of our income, we could help end&nbsp;
poverty and boost shared prosperity as well.&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.560
We still have so much more than many people in the&nbsp;
rest of the world who don't have the same social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:08.880
safety net, who don't have the same services&nbsp;
and baseline living standards that we have in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.440
countries like Germany and Australia.
A median income in a rich country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.960
like the UK or Australia puts you comfortably&nbsp;
within the top five percent richest people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:23.760
the world. And that money can improve the life of&nbsp;
someone in extreme poverty about 100 times more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.360
than it can improve your own life.
And it turns out, the pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.480
might just have been the push that&nbsp;
people needed to snap into action.&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:36.640
It has been quite heartening to see a lot of&nbsp;
people who do have more throughout this pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:41.360
take a moment and realize that now is a really&nbsp;
good time to be giving, especially those who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:45.920
benefited from things like a stock market rally.
And I've seen people really turn that into empathy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.720
and to compassion and go, yeah, if this is&nbsp;
what it's like for me, when things are actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:55.600
relatively good and the worst case isn't that bad,&nbsp;
I couldn't imagine being in a situation where it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.760
much worse, and I have seen that drive compassion,&nbsp;
and I really hope that is quite lasting.&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.280
The pandemic is likely going to be with us for&nbsp;
a while and its economic aftermath for an even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.720
longer time. It’s a global crisis that affects&nbsp;
all of us – and hence needs a global response.&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.320
That’s why the experts we talked to for this&nbsp;
report believe it’s a matter of self-interest - as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.640
well as a moral and humanitarian duty&nbsp;
- for the richer world to help those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.280
less fortunate. Otherwise the pandemic will&nbsp;
persist longer than it otherwise would,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.960
global inequalities will grow&nbsp;
even more, disparities will widen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:33.600
and there will be global divergence.
It's not just about inequality today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:49.840
it's also about what the current impacts can mean&nbsp;
for inequality and social mobility in the future.

